2?"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"shrink-0 text-eng-navy-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180","aria-hidden":"true","children":"▾"}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"border-t border-eng-navy-100 p-4","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-3 rounded-lg bg-eng-green-50 p-3 text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["Correct answer:"," "]}],"B. The store's business had declined and Ruth was reducing what she could keep up with."]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-[0.9375rem] leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Why: "}],"Three details in paragraph 2 point the same way: the display stops changing, hours are cut, and stock is not replaced. Together they indicate a business winding down."]}],[["$","p",null,{"className":"mb-2 mt-4 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-eng-navy-500","children":"Every choice, explained"}],["$","ul",null,{"className":"space-y-2","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-red-700","children":["A","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"Ruth had lost interest in her customers."}]," ","— ","Contradicted by the ending, where customers line up and Ruth stays open to sell through her stock."]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"text-sm leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"mr-1 font-bold text-eng-green-700","children":["B","."]}],["$","span",null,{"className":"italic text-eng-navy-500","children":"The store's business had declined and Ruth was reducing what she could keep up with."}]," ","— ","Correct. The unchanged display, reduced hours, and unfilled shelves are three separate signs of decline."]}],"$L35","$L36"]}]]]}]]}],"$L37","$L38"]}]]}],"$L39"]]}]]}]
1e:["$","section",null,{"id":"mastery-check","className":"scroll-mt-32 border-t border-eng-navy-200 pt-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-eng-navy-800 text-sm font-bold text-white","aria-hidden":"true","children":7}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-eng-teal-700","children":"Mastery Check"}]]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-2xl font-bold text-eng-navy-900 sm:text-3xl","children":"Check yourself"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-600","children":"A short formative check. It is not recorded — use it to decide whether to move on or review."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-700","children":"One short passage, four items. If you miss one, the review below each answer will tell you which part of the lesson to revisit."}],[["$","article","inf-mastery-p1",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-eng-navy-200 bg-white shadow-sm","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"border-b border-eng-navy-100 px-4 py-4 sm:px-6","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-3 gap-y-2","children":[["$","h4",null,{"className":"font-display text-lg font-bold text-eng-navy-900","children":"The Early Shift"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"rounded-full bg-eng-teal-50 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-semibold text-eng-teal-800","children":"Health"}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-xs text-eng-navy-400","children":[97," words"]}]]}]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"px-4 py-5 sm:px-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"max-w-[68ch] space-y-4 text-[1.0625rem] leading-[1.75] text-eng-navy-800","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"flex gap-3","children":["$undefined",["$","span",null,{"children":"Denise started a new job in September with a shift that began at 5 a.m. For the first two weeks she yawned through her morning break, and twice she fell asleep on the bus home and missed her stop. In the third week she moved her phone charger from her nightstand to the kitchen counter. She began laying out her work clothes before dinner instead of after the late news. By October she was reading on the bus again. Her sister, who had worked early shifts for years, noticed the charger on the counter and only smiled."}]]}]]}],"$undefined",["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 border-t border-eng-navy-100 pt-3 text-xs italic text-eng-navy-400","children":"Original passage — BEOC Academic Bridge"}]]}]]}]],["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","$L2d","inf-m1",{"question":{"id":"inf-m1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about Denise during her first two weeks at the new job?","passageId":"inf-mastery-p1","choices":["She did not know which bus stop was hers.","She was very tired.","She was unhappy with her new job.","She was bored at work."],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The passage never says Denise was tired. It shows you instead: she yawned through her break and twice fell asleep on the bus home. Add what you already know about people who do those things, and the inference is plain. If you missed this, revisit \"An inference is text plus what you already know\" in the lesson.","choiceRationales":["Misreads the detail. She missed her stop because she fell asleep, not because she did not know where to get off.","Correct. Yawning through her break and falling asleep on the bus are both signs of a tired person.","Not supported. The passage never says how Denise felt about the job itself.","Yawning alone could mean boredom, but falling asleep on the bus home happened away from work. Only tiredness explains both details."],"skill":"inferences","difficulty":"Foundation"},"number":1}],"$L3a","$L3b","$L3c"]}]]}]]}]
1f:["$","section",null,{"id":"write-it","className":"scroll-mt-32 border-t border-eng-navy-200 pt-10","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-2 flex items-center gap-3","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex h-9 w-9 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-eng-navy-800 text-sm font-bold text-white","aria-hidden":"true","children":8}],["$","span",null,{"className":"text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-eng-teal-700","children":"Write It"}]]}],["$","h2",null,{"className":"font-display text-2xl font-bold text-eng-navy-900 sm:text-3xl","children":"Now write it yourself"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-2 max-w-[68ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-eng-navy-600","children":"The same skill, going the other direction. Nothing here is submitted from this page — you will copy your paragraph into Brightspace when it is ready."}]]}],["$","$L3d",null,{"writeIt":{"lab":2,"taskName":"Claim plus textual proof","passageTitle":"Closing Time","prompt":["Nothing in Closing Time states that Ruth's business was failing. You concluded it anyway, from a window display that stopped changing, hours that got shorter, and shelves that did not refill. That is an inference: a conclusion the text supports without stating.","Write four to six sentences, each one following the pattern I can tell ___ because ___. Make four separate inferences about the store, Ruth, or the neighborhood, and prove each one with a specific detail from the passage.","The proof half is the graded half. Because it seems that way is not proof. Point at the sentence, the sign, the date, or the detail that put the idea in your head."],"checklist":["I make four separate inferences, not the same one four times","Every sentence names the specific detail that supports it","Each inference goes beyond what the passage states outright","No inference contradicts anything the passage says","I did not use outside knowledge about hardware stores"],"frames":["I can tell ______ because the passage says ______.","I can tell ______ because ______ appears right after ______.","The detail about ______ tells me ______, even though the passage never says so."],"model":{"intro":"Four inferences, four different details. Notice that the last one reaches furthest and is still tied to something on the page.","sentences":[{"text":"I can tell the store was in trouble months before it closed, because the window display stopped changing in March and the box fans were still up in July.","strand":"focus","note":"An inference with a date attached. The claim and its proof are both in one sentence, which is what the pattern is for."},{"text":"I can tell Ruth was cutting back rather than being forced out suddenly, because the first sign was hand-lettered and only changed the hours, while the printed sign came five months later.","strand":"evidence","note":"The proof is the contrast between the two signs — a detail most readers pass over. Specific beats obvious."},{"text":"I can tell she was not restocking, because the passage says the gaps in the back shelves did not fill in.","strand":"evidence","note":"Short, exact, and anchored to the passage's own wording. Not every inference needs to be elaborate."},{"text":"I can tell the store mattered to the neighborhood more than its sales showed, because the line on the last afternoon reached past the laundromat and included people who had not bought anything there in years.","strand":"organization","note":"The furthest reach, saved for last, and still nailed to two concrete details. Ordering inferences from safest to boldest is an organization choice."},{"text":"None of these are stated in the passage, but each one follows from what is.","strand":"conventions","note":"A clean closing sentence that names what the writer has been doing. Complete, correctly punctuated, and it earns its place."}]},"brightspace":{"week":2,"dropbox":"WRITING PORTFOLIO — Backfill Collection"}}}]]}]
